{"records":[{"id":"dfm3zy7h8z","title":"Targeting KRT17 inhibits ovarian cancer progression and platinum resistance by suppressing cancer stemness through the EPN1/Wnt/β -catenin axis----PART1","description":"Data for paper “KRT17 Promotes Ovarian Cancer Stemness，Progression, and Resistance to Platinum via Stabilization of EPN1 and Activation of the Wnt/β-catenin Pathway”---PARTI","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-05T13:18:29.517Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dfm3zy7h8z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dfm3zy7h8z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"G","last_name":"du","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gjxh7wrycw","title":"Exploiting The ASNS Vulnerability Gap: Five Decades of Selective Metabolic Starvation in Oncology","description":"This repository contains the Python scripts, source data, and visualization code used to generate the figures and diagrams featured in the comprehensive review manuscript titled \"Exploiting The ASNS Vulnerability Gap: Five Decades of Selective Metabolic Starvation in Oncology\" (or \"L-asparaginase in Oncology: Metabolic Vulnerabilities and Therapeutic Evolution\").\nThe dataset provides reproducible workflows for bibliometric trend analyses, text-mining term frequency calculations, therapeutic comparative matrices, and decision-pathway diagrams related to L-asparaginase (L-ASNase) research in acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma (ENKTCL), and solid tumors.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-15T11:01:42.105Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gjxh7wrycw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gjxh7wrycw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kh Abdullah","last_name":"Al-Aff","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Galib Muhammad Shahriar","last_name":"Himel","orcid":"0000-0002-2257-6751"},{"first_name":"Doaa Khalid","last_name":"Mezaal","orcid":"0009-0000-0333-7387"},{"first_name":"Kunnummal","last_name":"Sumithlal","orcid":"0009-0007-5282-9338"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmacology","Oncology","Cancer Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3kkc9yp8k7","title":"Mouse fetal liver/bone marrow transduced with NOTCH1-dE","description":"Acute leukemias are aggressive malignancies of developmentally arrested hematopoietic progenitors. We sought here to explore the possibility that changes in hematopoietic stem/progenitor cells during development might alter the biology of leukemias arising from this tissue compartment. Using a mouse model of acute T-cell leukemia, we found that leukemias generated from fetal liver (FL) and adult bone marrow (BM) differed dramatically in their leukemia stem cell activity with FL leukemias showing markedly reduced serial transplantability as compared to BM leukemias. We present evidence that this difference is due to NOTCH1-driven autocrine IGF1 signaling which is active in FL cells, but restrained in BM cells by EZH2-dependent H3K27 trimethylation. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-09-27T15:16:13.355Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3kkc9yp8k7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3kkc9yp8k7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Weng","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Fetal Development","Insulin-Like Growth Factor","Aging","T Cell","Lymphoid Malignancy","Notch","Mouse Model of Cancer","Transduction of Hematopoietic Stem Cells","Acute Leukemia"],"journal_issn":["19345909"]},{"id":"h772d6cx3f","title":"SynLethDB-Recon3D-A549","description":"The datasets associated with the article “Metabolic Synthetic Lethality–Guided Drug Repurposing for Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer: An Integrated Systems Biology and Molecular Modeling Study” are provided in three separate files, each corresponding to a distinct stage of the study.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-26T11:56:09.523Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/h772d6cx3f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/h772d6cx3f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sonia","last_name":"Sepahi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Computational Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r7tcjmb3nt","title":"Targeting KRT17 inhibits ovarian cancer progression and platinum resistance by suppressing cancer stemness through the EPN1/Wnt/β -catenin axis------ PARTII","description":"Paper \"KRT17 Promotes Ovarian Cancer Stemness\" Progression,  the second part of the original data of \"and Resistance to Platinum via Stabilization of EPN1 and Activation of the Wnt/β-catenin Pathway\" is the original data of animal experiments-PARTII","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-05T13:18:33.452Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r7tcjmb3nt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r7tcjmb3nt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"G","last_name":"du","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nf3hm5dj9m","title":"ALI data","description":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) is one of the neurotransmitters released by nonadrenergic noncholinergic inhibitory nerve endings. In lung tissues, VIP can inhibit alveolar macrophage phagocytosis and T lymphocyte proliferation, reduce the release of inflammatory mediators, and promote the repair of airway epithelial damage, thus playing an important role in treating pulmonary systemic diseases. This study aimed to explore the effects of VIP on lipopolysaccharide (LPS)-induced macrophage activation in vitro and LPS-induced acute lung injury (ALI) in mice. Mouse primary alveolar macrophage cells were treated with LPS (100 ng/mL) for 8 h and then co-incubated with different concentrations of VIP (108–106 mol/L) for 24 h. VIP treatment significantly inhibited LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and decreased the activation of the nuclear transcription factor-κB (NF-κB) signaling pathway. Subsequent studies confirmed that VIP-inhibited alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were dependent on the activation of Adenosine cyclic 3', 5'-monophosphate(cAMP)-protein kinase A(PKA)pathway. ALI was induced in mice by intraperitoneal (ip) injection of LPS (10 mg/kg), and different doses of VIP (1, 3, and 5 nmol/mouse, ip) were administered at 0, 8, and 16 h after LPS injection. VIP significantly reduced LPS-induced inflammatory cell infiltration, decreased the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-α, IL-1β, and IL-6), and eliminated LPS-mediated pulmonary edema. Also, LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were inhibited. In conclusion, VIP could effectively reduce the LPS-induced inflammatory responses in vitro and in vivo; this beneficial effect was associated with the activation of cAMP-PKA pathway and the inhibition of NF-κB signaling pathway activation, thereby reducing the expression of pro-inflammatory mediators and cytokines. These data suggest that VIP may be a potential drug for the treatment of ALI.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-31T06:29:14.599Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nf3hm5dj9m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nf3hm5dj9m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jingtao","last_name":"LI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Respiratory System"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ss6ghkd8bk","title":"Inhibition of M1 polarization in alveolar macrophages by vasoactive intestinal peptide attenuates LPS-induced acute lung injury in vivo and vitro","description":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) is one of the neurotransmitters released by nonadrenergic noncholinergic inhibitory nerve endings. In lung tissues, VIP can inhibit alveolar macrophage phagocytosis and T lymphocyte proliferation, reduce the release of inflammatory mediators, and promote the repair of airway epithelial damage, thus playing an important role in treating pulmonary systemic diseases. This study aimed to explore the effects of VIP on lipopolysaccharide (LPS)-induced macrophage activation in vitro and LPS-induced acute lung injury (ALI) in mice. Mouse primary alveolar macrophage cells were treated with LPS (100 ng/mL) for 8 h and then co-incubated with different concentrations of VIP (108–106 mol/L) for 24 h. VIP treatment significantly inhibited LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and decreased the activation of the nuclear transcription factor-κB (NF-κB) signaling pathway. Subsequent studies confirmed that VIP-inhibited alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were dependent on the activation of Adenosine cyclic 3', 5'-monophosphate(cAMP)-protein kinase A(PKA)pathway. ALI was induced in mice by intraperitoneal (ip) injection of LPS (10 mg/kg), and different doses of VIP (1, 3, and 5 nmol/mouse, ip) were administered at 0, 8, and 16 h after LPS injection. VIP significantly reduced LPS-induced inflammatory cell infiltration, decreased the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-α, IL-1β, and IL-6), and eliminated LPS-mediated pulmonary edema. Also, LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were inhibited. In conclusion, VIP could effectively reduce the LPS-induced inflammatory responses in vitro and in vivo; this beneficial effect was associated with the activation of cAMP-PKA pathway and the inhibition of NF-κB signaling pathway activation, thereby reducing the expression of pro-inflammatory mediators and cytokines. These data suggest that VIP may be a potential drug for the treatment of ALI.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-31T06:29:35.873Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ss6ghkd8bk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ss6ghkd8bk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"honggang","last_name":"wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lung Injury"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"p5gdc7mgbh","title":"Effects of voluntary wheel running on hippocampal synaptic remodeling in Rats With Autism Spectrum Disorder Induced by Valproic Acid(raw data)","description":"The hypothesis of this study is that autonomous running wheel exercise can improve valproic acid-induced behavioural deficits such as learning and memory ability, social impairment, novelty cognition in VPA male rats, and mediate the regulation of hippocampal synaptic remodelling through the classical Wnt pathway. The data include Morris water maze, three-box social experiment, new object recognition experiment, Niehl's staining, transmission electron microscopy, qRT-pcr and Western Blotting. tismhrough the data, we can find that the learning memory ability, social ability, novelty cognition of the autistic rats were significantly improved after the exercise intervention; the number of neurons was increased after the exercise, and the synapses were more stable and the synaptic plasticity (number of synapses, synapses, synapses and synapses) was increased. plasticity (synaptic number, synaptic gap, length of prominent active band, thickness of postsynaptic dense matter) were significantly improved; molecular biology experiments showed that the level of Dvl2,GSK3β, β-catenin protein in the classical Wnt pathway changed. It proved that the synaptic dysplasia was improved after exercise, and the over-activation state of classical Wnt pathway was suppressed.TEM and Western Blotting were counted by ImageJ software and then calculated together with the rest of the data by SPSS 23.0. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-17T19:33:09.798Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/p5gdc7mgbh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/p5gdc7mgbh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sichen","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Autism","Sport","Adapted Exercise"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"42sxkx9sdb","title":"Synthetic lethality-based prediction of anti-SARS-CoV-2 targets. Pal et al","description":"Supplementary data for the paper: \"Synthetic lethality-based prediction of anti-SARS-CoV-2 targets\". \nTable S1: Enriched Reactome pathways for DE gene sets (FDR<0.1). (Related to STAR Methods and Figure 2).\nTable S2: List of synthetic lethal pairs in each of the transcriptomic dataset used in this study and the list of unified 454 SL/SDL partner genes. (Related to STAR Methods and Figure 1).\nTable S3: List of 140 candidate targets and three subsets comprising known drug targets, hub SL/SDL partner genes when each one of them are paired up with multiple DE genes and genes with enriched Reactome pathways.  Pathway enrichment analysis (FDR <0.1) for SL/SDL partner genes, DE genes and combination pathway enrichment analysis for DE gene-SL/SDL partner gene pairs for 140 candidates. List of 135 candidates overlapping with genome-wide siRNA screen. (Related to STAR Methods and Figure 1 and 2).\nTable S4: Targeted siRNA screen results for selected 26 targets and annotation of 26 candidate targets with reference to SARS-CoV-2 or any other virus in the literature. (Related to STAR Methods and Figure 3).\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-04-19T18:44:35.774Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/42sxkx9sdb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/42sxkx9sdb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lipika","last_name":"R. Pal","orcid":"0000-0002-3390-110X"},{"first_name":"Kuoyuan","last_name":"Cheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nishanth Ulhas","last_name":"Nair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Martin-Sancho","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanju","last_name":"Sinha","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuan","last_name":"Pu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Riva","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fiorella","last_name":"Schischlik","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joo Sang","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sumit K.","last_name":"Chanda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eytan","last_name":"Ruppin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Transcriptome","Drug","Antiviral Therapy","CRISPR/Cas9","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7shf34snd3","title":"Data for Synthetic lethal connectivity and graph transformer improve synthetic lethality prediction","description":"Here stores the data we used to train and evaluate our MLEC-iSL model for individual sample-specific synthetic lethality prediction.\n\nThere are two types of features used in our model: omics features and biological networks. For omics features, there are two CCLE profiles (expression and essentiality) and four cell-specific features (expression, essentiality, mutation and copy number) considered in our model. As for network features, we incorporate physical protein-protein interaction (PPI) network, genetic interaction network and pathway network.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-13T15:52:41.149Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7shf34snd3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7shf34snd3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kunjie","last_name":"Fan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Systems Biology","Computational Biology","Deep Learning"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":142134,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":264,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":142134,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":141283,"restricted":71,"embargoed":780},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2358},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1341},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":709},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":478},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":446},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":429},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":418},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":403},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":334},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":278}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":139472},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1905},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":189},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":142},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":78},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":58},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":42},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":873},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":782},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":541},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":409},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":370}]}}